<p class="gna-lede">Most broken jaws are treated in one of two ways: <strong>closed reduction</strong>, where the teeth are guided into the correct bite and the jaws are held together with wires or elastics while the bone heals, or <strong>open reduction and internal fixation</strong>, where the surgeon repositions the bone and holds it with small titanium plates and screws. Plates are now widely used for displaced fractures because they usually let you open your mouth soon after surgery, but wiring still has an important place. The right choice depends on where the jaw is broken, how far it has moved, your teeth, your age and your general health.</p>

<h2 id="short-answer">Wiring or plates: the short answer</h2>
<p>Neither method suits every fracture. Wiring alone often works well for fractures that have moved very little, for many fractures near the jaw joint, and for children, whose bones heal and remodel quickly. Plates are usually preferred when the bone ends have shifted significantly, when there is more than one break, or when keeping the jaws closed for weeks would be unsafe or impractical. Many operations use both: the bite is temporarily wired during surgery to set the teeth in the right position, and then plates are fixed.</p>
<p>Large trials comparing every technique are limited, so experienced surgeons base the decision on the fracture pattern and the individual patient rather than on a single rule.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The main goal of treatment is a bite that fits together correctly once the bone has healed.</li>
    <li>Wiring (intermaxillary fixation, or IMF) holds the jaws together, usually for several weeks, with a liquid diet.</li>
    <li>Titanium plates hold the bone directly, usually through cuts inside the mouth, and allow earlier jaw movement.</li>
    <li>Fractures near the jaw joint (condylar fractures) are often treated without plates, but not always.</li>
    <li>Numbness of the lower lip is common after a jaw fracture and often improves over months.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="assessment">How a broken jaw is assessed</h2>
<p>In India, road-traffic accidents — particularly two-wheeler crashes — are a common cause of jaw fractures, along with falls, assaults and sports injuries. Typical signs include a bite that suddenly feels wrong, pain and swelling, difficulty opening the mouth, bleeding from the gums, loose teeth, and numbness of the lower lip or chin. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/broken-jaw-symptoms/">broken jaw symptoms</a> covers these in more detail.</p>
<p>At assessment, the surgeon first checks breathing, bleeding and any signs of head or neck injury, which always take priority. The face, teeth and bite are then examined, and imaging is arranged — usually a panoramic X-ray (OPG) and a CT scan, which shows the exact position of each break in three dimensions. The lower jaw often breaks in two places, so the whole jaw is checked, along with the cheekbones and eye sockets.</p>
<p>A fracture that runs through the tooth-bearing part of the jaw usually connects with the mouth through the gum, so antibiotics are commonly prescribed. Definitive treatment is generally planned within a few days, once swelling and any other injuries allow, though some situations need earlier temporary stabilisation.</p>

<h2 id="wiring">Closed reduction: jaw wiring (IMF)</h2>
<p>In closed reduction, the bone is not exposed. Instead, the surgeon uses the teeth to guide the broken pieces back into position. Metal bars are wired to the upper and lower teeth, or small screws are placed in the bone above and below the teeth, and the jaws are then held together with fine wires or strong elastics. This is called intermaxillary (or maxillomandibular) fixation — IMF.</p>
<p>It is usually done under general anaesthesia, sometimes under local anaesthesia for simple cases. For fractures of the main body of the lower jaw in adults, the jaws typically stay fixed for about four to six weeks while the bone knits together. Shorter periods are used for children and for many fractures near the joint.</p>
<h3>What life with IMF is like</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Liquid diet only</strong>, taken through a straw, a feeding cup or a syringe through gaps behind the teeth.</li>
  <li><strong>Speech is muffled</strong> but understandable; most people adapt within a few days.</li>
  <li><strong>Weight loss</strong> is common, so high-calorie, high-protein liquids are important.</li>
  <li><strong>Oral hygiene takes extra effort</strong>: rinsing after every meal and careful brushing of the outer tooth surfaces.</li>
  <li><strong>Wire cutters or scissors</strong> (for elastics) are usually kept with you at all times, and you will be shown when and how to use them in an emergency such as vomiting or breathing difficulty.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the fixation period ends, the bars or screws are removed, often as a short procedure, and jaw exercises help restore opening.</p>

<h2 id="plates">Open reduction: titanium plates</h2>
<p>In open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF), the surgeon exposes the fracture, lines up the bone ends with the teeth held in the correct bite, and fixes them with small titanium plates and screws. For most fractures of the front and body of the lower jaw, the incision is made <strong>inside the mouth</strong> through the gum, so there is no visible scar on the face. Some fractures towards the angle, the back of the jaw or the joint need a small incision on the skin.</p>
<p>The main advantage is that the bone is held firmly from the inside, so prolonged wiring is usually not needed. Most people can open their mouths, speak normally and eat a soft, non-chew diet within days of surgery. Light elastics are sometimes used for a short time to guide the bite.</p>
<p>Titanium is well tolerated by the body, and the plates are normally left in place permanently. A minority need removal later — for example because of infection, a plate becoming exposed through the gum, or discomfort. In older people with few or no teeth, the jaw bone can be very thin, and larger plates may be needed for stability.</p>

