<h2 id="comparison">Wiring vs plates at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Wiring (closed reduction / IMF)</th><th>Titanium plates (ORIF)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>How the bone is held</td><td>Indirectly, by fixing the teeth together</td><td>Directly, with plates and screws on the bone</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Incisions</td><td>None, or tiny punctures for IMF screws</td><td>Usually inside the mouth; sometimes a small skin incision</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Mouth opening after treatment</td><td>Jaws closed for the fixation period</td><td>Usually within days</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Diet</td><td>Liquids only for several weeks</td><td>Soft, non-chew diet, typically for about six weeks</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Often chosen for</td><td>Minimally displaced fractures, many condylar fractures, children</td><td>Displaced or multiple fractures, when long IMF is unsafe or impractical</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Main downsides</td><td>Weight loss, hygiene difficulty, airway concerns if vomiting, jaw stiffness afterwards</td><td>Surgical risks, possible need for later plate removal, nerve risk with skin approaches</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Long periods of IMF may be avoided in people with epilepsy, significant breathing problems, heavy alcohol use or a high risk of vomiting, or in people who could not manage a liquid diet safely. These factors are part of the discussion with your surgeon.</p>

<h2 id="condylar-fractures">What about condylar fractures?</h2>
<p>The condyle is the part of the lower jaw that forms the jaw joint, just in front of the ear. It often breaks from a blow to the chin — for example, falling forward from a two-wheeler — and may break on one or both sides. Signs include pain in front of the ear, the jaw swinging to one side on opening, and back teeth meeting early on one side with a gap on the other.</p>
<p>Many condylar fractures are managed <strong>closed</strong>: a short period of IMF, followed by guiding elastics and a structured exercise programme to restore movement. <strong>Open treatment</strong> with plates is considered when the fractured piece is significantly displaced or dislocated from the joint, when the height of the back of the jaw is shortened, or when both sides are broken and the front teeth no longer meet.</p>
<p>A meta-analysis comparing these approaches found few overall differences in outcomes, apart from less deviation of the jaw on opening after open treatment, and suggested closed treatment for minimally displaced fractures and open treatment for moderate to severe displacement. Open approaches carry a risk of weakness of the facial nerve, usually temporary, and a small skin scar. In children, condylar fractures are almost always treated without open surgery, because the growing joint remodels well; early movement is encouraged to reduce the risk of joint stiffness.</p>

<h2 id="recovery-and-diet">Recovery and diet</h2>
<p>Recovery varies with the fracture and the method used, but a typical course looks like this:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>First week:</strong> swelling and bruising peak at two to three days and then settle. Painkillers, antibiotics and mouth rinses are given as prescribed.</li>
  <li><strong>Weeks 1–2:</strong> most people return to desk work, depending on swelling, speech and any other injuries.</li>
  <li><strong>Weeks 4–6:</strong> initial bone healing; wires are usually removed in this window if IMF was used, and diet is gradually advanced after plating.</li>
  <li><strong>Around 3 months:</strong> the bone continues to strengthen. Contact sports are usually avoided for at least two to three months, or longer if your surgeon advises.</li>
</ul>
<p>For liquid diets, options include blended dal, soups, rasam, milk, lassi, buttermilk, ragi malt, thin kheer and nutritional supplement drinks. For soft, non-chew diets: idli soaked in sambar, curd rice, upma, pongal, mashed potato, scrambled eggs and well-cooked dal-rice. Our guide on <a href="/blogs/what-to-eat-after-jaw-surgery/">what to eat after jaw surgery</a> has more ideas.</p>
<p>Keep the mouth clean with gentle brushing and the rinses your team recommends. Avoid smoking and all tobacco, including gutka and khaini, during healing — tobacco reduces blood supply to the bone and increases the risk of infection.</p>

<h2 id="risks">Risks and complications</h2>
<p>Most jaw fractures heal well with appropriate treatment, but complications can occur with either method:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Infection</strong> of the fracture or the plates — more likely with delayed treatment, smoking, diabetes or teeth in the fracture line</li>
  <li><strong>A bite that does not fit perfectly</strong>, which may need adjustment or, occasionally, further surgery</li>
  <li><strong>Delayed healing or non-union</strong>, where the bone does not knit as expected</li>
  <li><strong>Numbness of the lower lip and chin</strong> from injury to the nerve inside the jaw — often caused by the fracture itself; it frequently improves over months but can be permanent</li>
  <li><strong>Facial nerve weakness</strong> after skin approaches, usually temporary</li>
  <li><strong>Plate problems</strong> such as loosening or exposure, needing removal</li>
  <li><strong>Jaw stiffness or jaw-joint pain</strong>, particularly after condylar fractures</li>
  <li><strong>Damage to teeth</strong> in or near the fracture line, sometimes requiring removal</li>
</ul>
<p>Your surgeon should explain which of these apply to your fracture and how they are managed. If other facial bones are also broken — such as the cheekbone — recovery may differ; see our guide to <a href="/blogs/cheekbone-fracture-zygomatic-signs-surgery-recovery/">cheekbone fractures</a>.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-seek-help">When to seek urgent help</h2>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department immediately</strong> if, after a facial injury, the person has difficulty breathing, heavy bleeding from the mouth or nose, drowsiness, confusion, repeated vomiting or a period of unconsciousness, or neck pain. These can signal life-threatening or head and spine injuries that come before jaw treatment.</p>
</div>
<p>Even without these signs, see a specialist the same day or as soon as possible if your teeth do not meet as they did before the injury, if you cannot open or close your mouth normally, or if your lip or chin is numb. Delaying treatment can make the fracture harder to set and raises the risk of infection. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/facial-trauma-first-aid-steps-everyone-must-know-in-emergencies/">facial trauma first aid</a> explains what to do while you get help.</p>
